 Flow uses the latest scheduled event's `awaiting` record, current review/rework events, and bounded read-only GitHub PR observations (a 30-second wall-clock budget per report, concurrent reads cached per unique PR). Flow observes native non-author GitHub approvals or commit-bound COMMENT reviews starting with `VERDICT: APPROVE` (optionally bold, case-insensitive), the fleet's shared-login review format. The latest explicit verdict supersedes older verdicts from that login; bots, old-head approvals and ordinary prose do not approve. This display signal is not ship authorization: independent reviewer-session custody is still verified by ship. The COMMENT verdict must start the first nonempty line; other approval-like prose is deliberately not interpreted. Known failing required checks, like pending checks, report `ci`. Missing checks or exhausted lookup budget never establish green; absent evidence stays `landing_unclassified`. A merged PR stays in deploy until there is explicit live readback. Deployment systems can persist `metadata.flow_pr: {url: <PR URL>, merged: true, deployed: true, verified: false}` on a task event to report live verification (`test`); this is observation only, not authority to merge or declare success. All three flags are strict booleans and the URL must match the card's PR. The command does not infer installation from merge time.
 
-`hermes kanban lanes` (default `show`) prints target against actual share of run-hours over `--hours` (default 6), running, ready, held and open cards per lane; on a synced board it reads the fleet replica unless `--local`. `lanes flow --json [--window 1h|6h|24h|7d]` lists every open card with step, reason and source plus per-lane done/run-hours/cost and today's cap usage. `held` and `unclassified` list cards; `assign <id> <lane>`, `admit <id>`, `pin <id> [--off]` and `set <lane> [--share N] [--admit-per-day N] [--cards-per-day N] [--usd-per-day N] [--pause|--resume]` (always written to the installation-root config) are the controls; a person's `admit` does not spend the daily budget (an agent's does); `promote` and `unblock` also release a held card and are not budgeted; `explain <id>` says which lane a card is in and why it waits. Unset `cards_per_day` / `usd_per_day` means unlimited. Over the cap, new cards wait at the gate with reason `limit:cards_per_day` or `limit:usd_per_day`; both reset at local midnight.
+`hermes kanban lanes` (default `show`) prints target against actual share of run-hours over `--hours` (default 6), running, ready, held and open cards per lane; on a synced board it uses canonical fleet accounting unless `--local`. In primary mode, open-card presentation selects one whole row/history/edge source per card: an unanswered local task/history/link edit keeps the local copy, otherwise a strictly newer fleet revision wins and ties keep local. Tombstones on either side suppress the deleted card. Strip and flow use this same selected set without merging source-local event/run IDs; run-hours, cost, throughput and daily budget totals remain canonical (not summed across copies). Shadow/off modes keep the prior replica presentation. `lanes flow --json [--window 1h|6h|24h|7d]` lists every open card with step, reason and source plus per-lane done/run-hours/cost and today's cap usage. `held` and `unclassified` list cards; `assign <id> <lane>`, `admit <id>`, `pin <id> [--off]` and `set <lane> [--share N] [--admit-per-day N] [--cards-per-day N] [--usd-per-day N] [--pause|--resume]` (always written to the installation-root config) are the controls; a person's `admit` does not spend the daily budget (an agent's does); `promote` and `unblock` also release a held card and are not budgeted; `explain <id>` says which lane a card is in and why it waits. Unset `cards_per_day` / `usd_per_day` means unlimited. Over the cap, new cards wait at the gate with reason `limit:cards_per_day` or `limit:usd_per_day`; both reset at local midnight.
 
 `hermes kanban lanes apply <file|->` applies a planner's JSON action list. Each action is validated on its own and refused with a reason when it breaks a limit: `admit` (at most `admit_per_day` per local day, never a `never_admit` title), `merge` (a held card into an open card of the same lane; it stays held), `reprioritize` (0 to 1000, never a pinned card), `relane` (only a card placed by inheritance or by default, never into or out of a gated lane) and `note`. Every action except `note` needs a one-line `reason`; one plan applies at most 50 actions.
 
